# A variant of scenario 2 of docs/reverse-engineering/verification-protocol.md:
# steering response, taken both ways in one run.
#
# Thrust throughout, with a half-second of full left, a straight, and then a
# half-second of full right. 200 ticks, matching the reference capture's length.
#
# Why both ways rather than the protocol's single constant turn: the sign of the
# yaw response is one of the things this harness exists to settle - row 0 of the
# recorded basis being the ship's *left* is an 84-confidence finding, and
# `oag-trace --basis` exists because of it. A run that turns one way and then the
# other is symmetric about the straight in the middle, so a sign error shows up
# as the two halves swapping rather than as a plausible-looking curve. A single
# constant turn cannot distinguish those.
#
# Why half a second and not longer: at the reference scenario's ~24 units/s and
# the ~1.5 rad/s yaw rate measured for a held steer, 30 ticks is about 43
# degrees. Long enough to be far outside any tolerance if the steering model is
# wrong, short enough that the ship should still be on Talon's Junction's first
# straight rather than in a wall - a scrape would confound the steering
# measurement with the collision response, which is scenario 4's job.
#
# The straight in the middle is not padding. It is where the ship's own damping
# is the only thing acting on the yaw, which is the part of the steering model a
# held input never exercises.
#
# Reference scenario: Time Trial, Venom, Talon's Junction White
# (Data\Environments\16_Track), Assegai.
#
# Reading a capture of this back tells you the input phase for free: the
# recorded `steer` column is flat until the tick the game first saw `left`, so
# the offset between "tick 60 of the script" and "the tick steer moves" is the
# emulator's input latency, measured rather than assumed. See
# `psp-trace.py --script-lead`.

60 cross
30 cross left
30 cross
30 cross right
50 cross
